L(4, '1. Follow attribute lookup before customizing it',
  'Distinguish instance storage, class attributes and fallback access.',
  [('Attribute lookup; __dict__', 'Ordinary attribute access can consult type-defined descriptors, instance storage and class inheritance. An instance dictionary is one storage mechanism, not the entire lookup protocol.'),
   ('__getattr__ versus __getattribute__', '__getattribute__ participates in ordinary access; __getattr__ is a fallback when normal access fails. Careless overrides can recurse or hide real missing attributes.')],
  "obj.name is more than a direct dictionary read. Python considers the attribute protocol and class hierarchy. Understanding that normal path helps you choose a property or descriptor instead of overriding every access.\n\nThe example shows a normal instance attribute, a class attribute and a narrow __getattr__ fallback for one virtual name. Unknown names still raise AttributeError. Returning a value for every spelling mistake would hide bugs and confuse tools such as hasattr.\n\nIf you override __getattribute__, using self.name inside that method can call the same override again. object.__getattribute__ provides a deliberate way to delegate ordinary access, but most domain validation should use simpler properties/descriptors. The next parts explain precedence and reusable validation.",
  'class Profile:\n    kind = "learner"\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n    def __getattr__(self, key):\n        if key == "label":\n            return self.name.upper()\n        raise AttributeError(key)\np = Profile("Asha")\nprint("Instance:", p.__dict__)\nprint("Class:", p.kind)\nprint("Virtual:", p.label)\nprint("Unknown exists:", hasattr(p, "typo"))',
  'Delegate a custom access override without recursively calling itself.',
  'class Value:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.number = 7\n    def __getattribute__(self, name):\n        return object.__getattribute__(self, name)\nprint(Value().number)',
  'Show that an ordinary instance attribute can shadow a plain class value.',
  'class Item:\n    kind = "class default"\na, b = Item(), Item()\na.kind = "instance override"\nprint(a.kind, b.kind, Item.kind)',
  [('Is __getattr__ the first handler for every normal attribute?', 'No; it is a fallback after normal lookup fails', 'Always', 'Only for slots', 'Normal lookup includes descriptors and instance/class storage.'),
   ('Why preserve AttributeError for genuinely unknown names?', 'To keep missing attributes and spelling mistakes visible', 'To disable all inheritance', 'To make every attribute immutable', 'Tools and callers rely on a truthful missing-attribute contract.')],
  'Return invented values for every missing attribute or recurse through self inside an access override.', 'Understand normal lookup and use narrow hooks only where needed.',
  'The virtual label uses a deliberate fallback; a typo still reports absence.',
  syntax='def __getattr__(self, name):\n    if name == supported_virtual_name: return value\n    raise AttributeError(name)')

L(4, '2. Distinguish data and non-data descriptor precedence',
  'Predict whether an instance dictionary can shadow a class-defined descriptor.',
  [('Descriptor; __get__', 'An object stored on a class can customize access through __get__(instance, owner). Descriptor behavior is found on its type.'),
   ('Data descriptor', 'Defines __set__ or __delete__ as well as any read behavior. In ordinary lookup it takes precedence over an instance dictionary entry with the same public name.'),
   ('Non-data descriptor', 'Defines __get__ without __set__/__delete__. An instance dictionary entry can shadow it. Ordinary Python functions are an important example.')],
  "A descriptor lives on the class, not merely in an instance dictionary. Its methods specify how access or assignment behaves for instances. Data and non-data are protocol categories; the word data does not mean the descriptor itself stores every instance's value.\n\nFor the ordinary object lookup path, a data descriptor takes precedence over same-name instance storage. Instance storage takes precedence over a non-data descriptor or plain class value. Class inheritance determines which class attribute is found, and custom access overrides can alter normal behavior.\n\nThe example deliberately writes conflicting instance dictionary entries. The data descriptor still controls its public read, while the non-data descriptor is shadowed. This is protocol behavior, not a security boundary: descriptor backing storage may remain deliberately accessible.",
  'class NonData:\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner=None):\n        return "non-data descriptor"\nclass Data:\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner=None):\n        return "data descriptor"\n    def __set__(self, instance, value):\n        instance.__dict__["backing"] = value\nclass Demo:\n    ordinary = NonData()\n    controlled = Data()\nd = Demo()\nd.__dict__["ordinary"] = "instance shadow"\nd.__dict__["controlled"] = "attempted shadow"\nprint("Non-data read:", d.ordinary)\nprint("Data read:", d.controlled)',
  'Show a plain instance attribute shadows an ordinary class attribute.',
  'class Example:\n    number = 1\ne = Example()\ne.__dict__["number"] = 9\nprint(e.number, Example.number)',
  'Confirm that putting a descriptor-like object only on an instance does not invoke its class-storage protocol.',
  'class Marker:\n    def __get__(self, instance, owner=None):\n        return "descriptor access"\nclass Host:\n    pass\nh = Host()\nmarker = Marker()\nh.value = marker\nprint("Stored object itself:", h.value is marker)',
  [('Which wins in ordinary lookup: a data descriptor or a same-name instance dictionary entry?', 'The data descriptor', 'Always the instance entry', 'Whichever was assigned last', 'Data descriptors have priority in the standard lookup path.'),
   ('Can an instance dictionary shadow a non-data descriptor?', 'Yes', 'No', 'Only if it returns None', 'Non-data descriptors sit below instance storage in ordinary lookup.')],
  'Assume every attribute is a direct dictionary lookup or every descriptor has the same precedence.', 'Identify the descriptor category and the normal lookup order.',
  'The conflicting names produce different reads because their descriptor categories differ.',
  syntax='data descriptor -> instance storage -> non-data descriptor/class value -> missing fallback')

L(4, '3. Validate assignments with a reusable descriptor',
  'Store values per instance and reject invalid later assignments, not only constructor inputs.',
  [('__set_name__', 'Called during normal class creation to tell an assigned descriptor its owner and attribute name. It can prepare a private storage name.'),
   ('NonNegativeInt', 'The project data descriptor validates ordinary nonnegative int assignments, rejects bool, and stores each value under that instance\'s underscore name.'),
   ('Class access branch', '__get__ receives instance=None for class access. Returning the descriptor itself permits useful inspection of its configuration.'),
   ('Event', 'The supplied slotted record accepts an id, a declared category and nonnegative minutes. Its public id/category properties are read-only; public minutes assignments use NonNegativeInt.')],
  "A property is convenient for one field. A reusable descriptor can apply the same assignment policy across several classes or fields without duplicating setter logic. Give each field its own descriptor instance unless shared configuration is intentional.\n\nThe descriptor object belongs to the class, but the values belong to individual instances. Storing the current value on self inside the descriptor would accidentally make every instance share that one value. Our implementation instead uses the instance's backing attribute.\n\nValidation applies when minutes is assigned after construction as well. Rejecting bool is deliberate because Python bool is an int subclass. Underscore backing attributes remain accessible by design; this is an API validation convention, not protection against hostile code modifying internals.",
  'from streamkit import Event, NonNegativeInt\na, b = Event("a", "study", 25), Event("b", "review", 10)\na.minutes = 30\nprint("Independent values:", a.minutes, b.minutes)\nprint("Class access returns descriptor:", isinstance(Event.minutes, NonNegativeInt))\ntry:\n    a.minutes = True\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Boolean minutes rejected")\nprint("Previous valid value:", a.minutes)',
  'Use the same descriptor class for a different domain field.',
  'from streamkit import NonNegativeInt\nclass Task:\n    attempts = NonNegativeInt()\n    def __init__(self, attempts):\n        self.attempts = attempts\na, b = Task(2), Task(4)\na.attempts = 3\nprint(a.attempts, b.attempts)\nprint("Backing name:", Task.attempts.storage)',
  'Demonstrate invalid assignment leaves the previous valid value intact.',
  'from streamkit import Event\ne = Event("a", "study", 5)\ntry:\n    e.minutes = -1\nexcept ValueError:\n    pass\nprint("Still valid:", e.minutes)',
  [('Where should per-instance validated values live?', 'In each instance backing storage', 'In one shared descriptor value field', 'Only in a global current value', 'A class-owned descriptor must not accidentally share instance data.'),
   ('Is underscore backing storage a security boundary?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when slots are present', 'The protocol expresses an API, not hostile-code isolation.')],
  'Store all instances values on the shared descriptor itself.', 'Keep descriptor configuration on the class and each value on its instance.',
  'Changing a does not change b, and rejected assignment preserves the earlier valid value.',
  syntax='def __set__(self, instance, value):\n    validate(value)\n    setattr(instance, self.storage_name, value)')

L(4, '4. Understand method binding as descriptor behavior',
  'Connect functions, bound methods and properties to ordinary class attribute access.',
  [('Bound method; __self__; __func__', 'Accessing an ordinary function through an instance creates a method combining that instance and original function. Calling it supplies the stored instance as self.'),
   ('property', 'A familiar data-descriptor interface for getters/setters. A property without a setter still controls assignment rather than becoming freely shadowable instance data.')],
  "self is not a magical keyword inserted into every function call. An ordinary function stored on a class implements descriptor binding. Its access through an instance returns a bound method carrying both the instance and function.\n\nCalling Counter.add(counter, amount) and calling counter.add(amount) follow the same underlying function with the instance supplied differently. Store a bound method and it also retains a reference to its instance, which matters for object lifetime.\n\nProperties use the same broader protocol to turn an attribute read into a getter call. Read-only properties prevent ordinary public assignment but do not imply deep object immutability or protect underscore implementation details.",
  'class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 0\n    def add(self, amount):\n        self.value += amount\n        return self.value\nc = Counter()\nmethod = c.add\nfunction = Counter.__dict__["add"]\nprint("Bound instance:", method.__self__ is c)\nprint("Original function:", method.__func__ is function)\nprint("Bound call:", method(3))\nprint("Explicit function call:", function(c, 2))',
  'Bind a function explicitly through its __get__ protocol.',
  'class Greeting:\n    def say(self, name):\n        return "Hello " + name\ng = Greeting()\nf = Greeting.__dict__["say"]\nbound = f.__get__(g, Greeting)\nprint(bound("Asha"))',
  'Show a read-only property rejects public assignment.',
  'class Label:\n    @property\n    def name(self):\n        return "fixed API label"\nx = Label()\nprint(x.name)\ntry:\n    x.name = "new"\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print("Read-only public property")',
  [('What does a bound method retain?', 'The instance and underlying function', 'Only the function name text', 'Only the latest return value', 'Those references determine its later invocation.'),
   ('Does a read-only property make the entire object deeply immutable?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if it returns a string', 'Other attributes or contained objects may still change.')],
  'Treat self binding as unrelated magic or equate read-only access with total immutability.', 'Follow the descriptor and reference behavior behind the familiar syntax.',
  'Both calling forms reach the same function and update the same counter instance.',
  syntax='instance.method(argument)\n# underlying relationship: Class.method(instance, argument)')

L(4, '5. Prefer class-creation hooks for ordinary registration',
  'Use __init_subclass__ and understand when field-name hooks run.',
  [('__init_subclass__', 'A base-class hook run when subclasses are created. It can validate/register subclasses and should cooperate with super when appropriate.'),
   ('Definition-time registration', 'Registration occurs while the class definition executes, not when its first instance is constructed. Import-time side effects should remain deliberate and bounded.')],
  "A registry of named processing stages rarely needs a custom metaclass. A base class can validate a subclass keyword and register the new class in __init_subclass__. This keeps the extension point close to the domain API.\n\nDescriptor __set_name__ runs as part of normal class creation before the parent subclass hook. A class decorator runs after the completed class is produced. Hooks therefore observe different stages; do not assume decorator-added fields existed during the earlier subclass hook.\n\nRegistration dictionaries are mutable state. Duplicate-name policy, import order and deliberate module reloading deserve attention in a real plugin system. The small isolated example rejects duplicate names and does not scan folders or import unknown external code.",
  'registry = {}\nclass Stage:\n    def __init_subclass__(cls, *, name, **kwargs):\n        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)\n        if name in registry:\n            raise ValueError("duplicate stage")\n        registry[name] = cls\nclass Normalize(Stage, name="normalize"):\n    pass\nclass Summarize(Stage, name="summarize"):\n    pass\nprint("Registered:", sorted(registry))\nprint("Class stored:", registry["normalize"] is Normalize)',
  'Trace descriptor naming before the parent subclass hook.',
  'trace = []\nclass Field:\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        trace.append("set_name " + name)\nclass Parent:\n    def __init_subclass__(cls):\n        super().__init_subclass__()\n        trace.append("parent hook")\nclass Child(Parent):\n    value = Field()\nprint(trace)',
  'Observe that class decorators run after the subclass hook.',
  'trace = []\nclass Base:\n    def __init_subclass__(cls):\n        super().__init_subclass__()\n        trace.append("subclass hook")\ndef decorate(cls):\n    trace.append("class decorator")\n    return cls\n@decorate\nclass Child(Base):\n    pass\nprint(trace)',
  [('Do you need a metaclass merely to register ordinary subclasses?', 'Usually no; __init_subclass__ can be sufficient', 'Always', 'Only if names are strings', 'Use the simplest hook that fits the requirement.'),
   ('Does instance construction trigger the first subclass registration here?', 'No; class creation does', 'Always', 'Only after importing json', 'The hook runs when the subclass is produced.')],
  'Introduce a metaclass for a job a simple class hook already expresses.', 'Choose the narrowest class-creation extension point and test its timing.',
  'Registration completes before any instances are created. The traces expose hook order.',
  syntax='class Stage:\n    def __init_subclass__(cls, *, name, **kwargs):\n        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)\n        registry[name] = cls')

L(4, '6. Read the metaclass creation path carefully',
  'Explain type, namespace preparation and class hooks without making metaclasses a default tool.',
  [('Metaclass; type', 'A metaclass constructs class objects. Ordinary Python classes commonly have type as their metaclass; type(name, bases, namespace) can construct a class dynamically.'),
   ('__prepare__; metaclass __new__/__init__', 'Preparation supplies the class-body namespace. The metaclass creates and initializes the class; calling type.__new__ performs descriptor naming and parent subclass hooks.'),
   ('Metaclass compatibility', 'Inheritance constrains which metaclass can construct a class. Custom metaclasses can conflict, which is another reason to prefer narrower hooks when sufficient.')],
  "A class statement executes a body into a namespace and asks a metaclass to construct a class object. That is different from calling the finished class to make an instance. A custom metaclass can inspect class-level definitions, but it is a specialized extension point.\n\nThe trace uses a normal dictionary namespace, records metaclass preparation/creation, lets type.__new__ perform descriptor naming and the parent hook, then records metaclass initialization and a class decorator. Predict the order before reading the executed output.\n\nFor field naming, validation and ordinary subclass registration, prefer __set_name__, properties/descriptors or __init_subclass__. Use a metaclass only when control over class construction itself is justified. This lesson deliberately avoids a complicated plugin framework or unsupported promises about every custom lookup path.",
  'trace = []\nclass Field:\n    def __set_name__(self, owner, name):\n        trace.append("descriptor named")\nclass Base:\n    def __init_subclass__(cls):\n        super().__init_subclass__()\n        trace.append("parent subclass hook")\nclass Meta(type):\n    @classmethod\n    def __prepare__(mcls, name, bases):\n        trace.append("prepare namespace")\n        return {}\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        trace.append("metaclass new")\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)\n    def __init__(cls, name, bases, namespace):\n        super().__init__(name, bases, namespace)\n        trace.append("metaclass init")\ndef decorate(cls):\n    trace.append("class decorator")\n    return cls\n@decorate\nclass Example(Base, metaclass=Meta):\n    field = Field()\nprint(trace)\nprint("Metaclass:", type(Example).__name__)',
  'Create a small class dynamically with type.',
  'Dynamic = type("Dynamic", (), {"kind": "example"})\nprint(Dynamic.__name__, Dynamic().kind)\nprint("Ordinary metaclass:", type(Dynamic) is type)',
  'Choose the narrower hook for a field-name notification.',
  'print("Need to learn a descriptor field name: __set_name__")\nprint("Need ordinary subclass registration: __init_subclass__")\nprint("Need class-construction control: consider a justified metaclass")',
  [('Is a metaclass call creating a class the same event as creating its instance?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for type', 'Class construction and later instance construction are different layers.'),
   ('Which standard operation runs descriptor naming and the parent subclass hook here?', 'type.__new__ inside metaclass creation', 'The later first instance call', 'Only the class decorator', 'The normal class creation path performs those hooks before final decoration.')],
  'Use a metaclass by default or confuse class construction with instance initialization.', 'Trace the normal creation path and choose a narrower hook when it suffices.',
  'The trace makes the creation order visible without using interpreter-dependent memory addresses.',
  syntax='class Meta(type):\n    def __new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace):\n        return super().__new__(mcls, name, bases, namespace)',
  diagram=('Normal class creation with a custom metaclass', ['Prepare namespace; execute class body', 'Metaclass __new__ calls type.__new__', 'Descriptors receive __set_name__', 'Parent receives __init_subclass__', 'Metaclass initialization; then class decorators'], 'The graphic summarizes this declared normal creation path. Alternative metaclasses can change behavior; ordinary registration often needs only __init_subclass__.'))

L(5, '1. Model names as references rather than storage boxes',
  'Separate identity, equality, aliasing and rebinding.',
  [('Reference; alias; rebinding', 'A name refers to an object. Assigning another name can create an alias; rebinding one name does not mutate the earlier object.'),
   ('is versus ==', 'is tests identity. == follows value-equality behavior. Equal objects can be distinct, and identity should not be inferred from implementation caching.')],
  "a = b does not copy the object referred to by b. Both names can refer to the same mutable list. Mutating it through one alias is visible through the other. Rebinding one name to a new list changes that name's reference, not the old list.\n\nShallow copying duplicates a container but keeps references to its contained objects. Nested mutation can therefore still be shared. Deep copying has a different contract and can be costly or inappropriate for resources; use it only when the intended object graph justifies it.\n\nDo not rely on small-integer or string interning accidents to test value equality with is. Use == for values and is for identities such as a unique sentinel or None. The example uses lists to make identities and mutations unambiguous.",
  'a = [[1], [2]]\nb = a\nc = a.copy()\nprint("Alias identity:", a is b)\nprint("Shallow copy identity:", a is c)\nc[0].append(9)\nprint("Nested shared mutation:", a)\nb = ["new object"]\nprint("Rebinding preserved old object:", a)\nprint("Equal but distinct:", [1, 2] == [1, 2], [1, 2] is [1, 2])',
  'Use deepcopy for a small intentionally independent nested fixture.',
  'from copy import deepcopy\na = [[1]]\nb = deepcopy(a)\nb[0].append(2)\nprint("Original:", a)\nprint("Copy:", b)',
  'Show that deleting one name does not remove another alias.',
  'a = [1, 2]\nb = a\ndel a\nprint("Still reachable:", b)',
  [('Does assigning a second name automatically clone a mutable object?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for lists', 'Both references can point to the same object.'),
   ('Does a shallow container copy recursively clone every child?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with copy()', 'Nested references remain shared unless separately copied.')],
  'Use is for value equality or assume assignment/deletion controls every reference.', 'Trace the reachable object graph and the exact operation performed.',
  'Nested mutation demonstrates shared child references, while rebinding affects only one name.',
  syntax='alias = original\nshallow = original.copy()\n# alias shares the object; shallow shares child references')

L(5, '2. Separate object size, lifetime and resource cleanup',
  'Interpret shallow sizing and avoid relying on garbage collection for file ownership.',
  [('sys.getsizeof', 'Reports a shallow object size under the interpreter. It does not recursively include every referenced value and is not whole-process memory usage.'),
   ('Reference counting; cyclic garbage collection', 'CPython commonly combines reference counting with a cyclic collector. Object lifetime and collection details are implementation-specific; del removes a binding, not a universal immediate-destruction command.'),
   ('weakref.ref', 'Observes an object without keeping it alive through a normal strong reference. Not every object layout supports weak references.')],
  "A list can be small according to getsizeof while referring to large strings or other objects. Adding shallow sizes naively can also count shared children more than once. Name exactly what quantity you measured before drawing a memory conclusion.\n\nAn unreachable cycle can retain references within itself even after its external name is deleted. CPython's cyclic collector can reclaim suitable unreachable objects. The example asks for collection and checks a weak observation without depending on the exact number of unrelated collected objects.\n\nFile handles and transactions need deterministic scope ownership, not guesses about collection timing or finalizers. Use with and reliable cleanup as on Day 3. A collector is not a replacement for a documented resource lifetime.",
  'import gc\nimport sys\nimport weakref\nlarge = "x" * 100000\ncontainer = [large]\nprint("List shallow bytes:", sys.getsizeof(container))\nprint("Referenced string bytes:", sys.getsizeof(large))\nclass Node:\n    pass\nnode = Node()\nnode.link = node\nobservation = weakref.ref(node)\ndel node\ngc.collect()\nprint("Unreachable cycle collected:", observation() is None)',
  'Show two container entries can refer to one shared object.',
  'import sys\nvalue = "x" * 1000\nitems = [value, value]\nprint("Shared child:", items[0] is items[1])\nprint("Shallow list bytes:", sys.getsizeof(items))\nprint("Do not count the shared child twice in a deep-size estimate")',
  'Keep resource cleanup explicit rather than relying on del.',
  'import io\nwith io.StringIO("owned") as handle:\n    print(handle.read())\nprint("Closed by scope:", handle.closed)',
  [('Does getsizeof(list_object) recursively count all referred strings?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if the strings are long', 'It is a shallow measurement.'),
   ('Should a file close depend on when garbage collection happens?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only on CPython', 'Explicit scope ownership is the portable reliable resource contract.')],
  'Call a shallow size complete memory or depend on collection timing for files.', 'Label sizing scope and keep deterministic resource cleanup.',
  'The list and child have separate shallow sizes. The cycle check uses a weak observation after an explicit collection request.',
  syntax='shallow_bytes = sys.getsizeof(object)\n# not recursive graph size and not process RSS')

L(5, '3. Use slots with their real limitations',
  'Restrict ordinary instance attributes without claiming immutability or guaranteed speed.',
  [('__slots__', 'Declares instance storage slots and can avoid a normal instance dictionary in suitable classes. It can reduce per-instance overhead for many fixed-shape objects.'),
   ('Inherited __dict__; __weakref__ slot', 'Slots do not remove a dictionary inherited from a base. Weak-reference support may require an explicit __weakref__ slot when it is not inherited.')],
  "A fixed-shape record can benefit from slots when many instances are alive at once. Attribute names become declared layout entries rather than arbitrary keys in each instance dictionary. The savings depend on interpreter and actual class hierarchy.\n\nSlots do not make permitted fields immutable. They also do not erase a base class's instance dictionary, freeze nested values, validate every assigned value or guarantee faster code. The Event record combines slots with a separate descriptor because layout and validation solve different problems.\n\nWeak references and multiple inheritance need deliberate layout design. The examples show a clean slotted class, a subclass that still inherits a dictionary, and explicit weak-reference support. Measure the workload rather than assuming slots always produce the same benefit.",
  'class Point:\n    __slots__ = ("x", "y")\n    def __init__(self, x, y):\n        self.x, self.y = x, y\np = Point(1, 2)\np.x = 9\nprint("Existing field mutable:", p.x)\nprint("Instance dict:", hasattr(p, "__dict__"))\ntry:\n    p.extra = 7\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print("Undeclared ordinary attribute rejected")',
  'Show an inherited instance dictionary remains available.',
  'class Base:\n    pass\nclass Child(Base):\n    __slots__ = ("x",)\nc = Child()\nc.extra = 5\nprint("Inherited dict:", c.__dict__)',
  'Declare weak-reference support explicitly in a small slotted class.',
  'import weakref\nclass Item:\n    __slots__ = ("value", "__weakref__")\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 1\nx = Item()\nprint("Weak observation:", weakref.ref(x)() is x)',
  [('Do slots make existing fields immutable?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if the field is numeric', 'Storage layout is different from assignment/immutability policy.'),
   ('Can a slotted child inherit an instance dictionary?', 'Yes', 'No', 'Only with no fields', 'A dictionary-bearing base can preserve that storage capability.')],
  'Advertise slots as universal immutability, security or speed.', 'Check inheritance and storage needs, then measure the intended workload.',
  'The permitted x field changes normally; only the undeclared attribute is rejected in the clean layout.',
  syntax='class FixedShape:\n    __slots__ = ("field_a", "field_b")')

L(5, '4. Design a controlled layout experiment',
  'Hold object counts and values equal and distinguish traced bytes from process RSS.',
  [('tracemalloc; current and peak', 'Tracks many Python allocator operations after tracing begins. Current bytes describe live traced allocations; peak is the highest traced amount during that session.'),
   ('compare_layouts', 'The supplied helper launches a fresh owned process for each normal/slotted layout, keeps equal batches alive and records interpreter, count, current and peak traced bytes.'),
   ('Controlled comparison', 'Both layouts use two fields and the same shared zero values. The reference list is included; class definitions happen before tracing. Results are workload/interpreter-specific.')],
  "A meaningful comparison needs the same object count, payload and lifetime. Measuring one plain instance against a million slotted instances answers nothing useful. A separate process per layout also avoids comparing one retained batch with another while both coexist.\n\nThe helper starts tracing after class setup, creates the reference list and instances, and captures current/peak bytes while every instance remains reachable. Values are shared zeros in both layouts, so this primarily compares list and instance storage under that workload.\n\nTraced Python allocations are not complete OS resident memory, every native allocation or runtime speed. Report the interpreter, version, record count and scope. The full million-object measurement follows; this smaller run first checks the procedure without imposing a universal expected byte count.",
  'from memory_lab import compare_layouts\nr = compare_layouts(10000)\nprint("Interpreter:", r["implementation"], r["python"])\nprint("Objects each:", r["objects_per_layout"])\nfor row in r["measurements"]:\n    print(row["kind"], "live bytes", row["current_bytes"], "peak bytes", row["peak_bytes"])\nprint("Both counts equal:", len({row["count"] for row in r["measurements"]}) == 1)',
  'Check the measured peak is not below the live snapshot for either layout.',
  'from memory_lab import compare_layouts\nr = compare_layouts(1000)\nprint("Valid peak relation:", all(row["peak_bytes"] >= row["current_bytes"] for row in r["measurements"]))',
  'List quantities this experiment does not measure.',
  'for quantity in ["whole-process RSS", "all native allocations", "recursive graph size", "runtime speedup", "all possible interpreter builds"]:\n    print(quantity)',
  [('Why keep each batch alive until after the snapshot?', 'To compare live equal-size object populations', 'To force a timing speedup', 'To remove the reference list', 'Already destroyed objects would not represent the intended live layout.'),
   ('Is tracemalloc current_bytes a complete process RSS measurement?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for a million objects', 'The measurement covers traced allocation scope rather than the whole process.')],
  'Compare unequal payloads or publish traced bytes as universal process memory.', 'Control the batch and label the measured quantity precisely.',
  'Each layout is measured in its own tracing session and process with the same number of live objects.',
  syntax='tracemalloc.start()\nobjects = [record_type(0, 0) for _ in range(count)]\ncurrent, peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()')

L(5, '5. Assemble a clean streaming-library handoff',
  'Connect protocols, validation, scopes, instrumentation and reconstructable output.',
  [('write_report', 'The local project creates a synthetic input by default, streams selected validated events, hashes the CSV and writes a report including a separately measured layout experiment.'),
   ('Artifact consistency', 'A saved report and CSV can be checked against one another using row totals and a digest. This is evidence of agreement, not an automatic multi-file transaction or source signature.'),
   ('file_digest', 'The local helper reads fixed-size binary blocks and returns the CSV SHA-256 fingerprint without loading the whole file into memory.')],
  "The milestone library gives each mechanism one job. Iterators and generators supply demand-driven records. Context managers own resources and output replacement. A decorator instruments the synchronous parser. A descriptor validates minutes assignments, while slots define the record shape.\n\nThe public API states strict headers, categories and digit-format rules, propagation behavior and bounded-state assumptions. Tests cover late errors, early exit, metadata and previous-output preservation. These contracts matter more than adding a custom metaclass the library does not need.\n\nThe report separates streaming results from the intentionally eager memory experiment. Verify selected row counts, per-category minute totals and the CSV digest. If writing the report fails after CSV replacement, the pair may be temporarily inconsistent; the documented verification detects that limitation rather than hiding it.",
  'import csv\nfrom lab import write_report, file_digest\nr = write_report("artifacts", count=30, memory_count=2000)\nwith open("artifacts/selected.csv", encoding="utf-8", newline="") as handle:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(handle))\nprint("Parsed / written:", r["parsed"], r["written"])\nprint("Small-fixture row parity:", len(rows) == r["written"])\nprint("Minute parity:", sum(int(row["minutes"]) for row in rows) == sum(r["minutes"].values()))\nprint("Digest parity:", file_digest("artifacts/selected.csv") == r["selected_csv_sha256"])',
  'Inspect report consistency on a tiny complete run.',
  'import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom lab import write_report\nr = write_report("small", count=6, memory_count=1000)\nsaved = json.loads(Path("small/report.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))\nprint("Parsed:", saved["parsed"])\nprint("Written:", saved["written"])\nprint("Totals:", saved["minutes"])',
  'Name which project mechanism owns each responsibility.',
  'roles = {"generation": "iter_events/select_categories/as_rows", "resource lifetime": "open_events and atomic_text", "call instrumentation": "count_calls", "assignment validation": "NonNegativeInt", "storage layout": "Event.__slots__"}\nfor role, mechanism in roles.items():\n    print(role + ": " + mechanism)',
  [('Does this library need a custom metaclass to meet its current contract?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only because it reads CSV', 'Simpler protocols and hooks already express its requirements.'),
   ('Does the saved CSV hash make two file replacements transactional?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with SHA-256', 'It detects byte disagreement against the report, not atomicity of the pair.')],
  'Add advanced machinery without a concrete responsibility or hide partial multi-file updates.', 'Ship a small API with tested behavior, reconstructable evidence and explicit limits.',
  'Only the tiny verification fixture is materialized in this example; the actual writer remains streaming.',
  syntax='report = write_report(output_folder)\nverify_counts_totals_and_csv_digest(report)')

L(5, '6. Milestone: a traceable streaming library',
  'Complete the documented library and measure one million objects per layout honestly.',
  [('One-million-object workload', 'The main example holds 1,000,000 two-field instances per layout in separate processes. Both use the same shared values; the list and traced instance storage are included.'),
   ('Measured evidence versus universal claim', 'A recorded byte difference describes this interpreter/build/workload. It is not a fixed saving for every class, a speedup, or measured streaming-pipeline memory.')],
  "Finish by running the complete CSV project and the controlled million-object comparison. Inspect the library source before using each protocol: explicit iterator state, pull-based generators, deterministic context ownership, wraps-preserving instrumentation and per-instance descriptor storage.\n\nThe worked example reports the actual live traced allocation of each equal-size layout and plots that measurement. It does not manufacture a promised percentage reduction. Keep the version, scope, shared payload and separate-process design alongside the result so another learner can reproduce or explain a difference.\n\nThe practical handoff is the code, tests, README, selected CSV and report with its digest and limits. No Study Hub progress is used as input. Week 50 builds on these ownership and state ideas to discuss concurrency; our counters and file pair are not advertised as concurrent transactions.",
  'import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom memory_lab import compare_layouts\nr = compare_layouts(1_000_000)\nrows = r["measurements"]\nvalues = [row["current_bytes"]/(1024*1024) for row in rows]\nprint("Interpreter:", r["implementation"], r["python"])\nprint("Objects per layout:", r["objects_per_layout"])\nfor row in rows:\n    print(row["kind"], "live bytes", row["current_bytes"], "peak bytes", row["peak_bytes"])\nprint("Live traced difference:", rows[0]["current_bytes"]-rows[1]["current_bytes"])\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.3, 3.3))\nax.bar(["Normal instance", "Slotted instance"], values, color=["#247ba0", "#cf6e27"])\nax.set(ylabel="Live traced allocation (MiB)", title="1,000,000 equal two-field objects per fresh process")\nax.text(0.5, 0.96, r["implementation"] + " " + r["python"] + "; list included; shared zeros", transform=ax.transAxes, ha="center", va="top", fontsize=9, bbox={"facecolor": "white", "alpha": 0.9, "edgecolor": "none"})\nfig.tight_layout()\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)',
  'Repeat a smaller comparison and calculate a clearly labeled per-object batch average.',
  'from memory_lab import compare_layouts\nr = compare_layouts(10000)\nfor row in r["measurements"]:\n    print(row["kind"], "batch average traced bytes", round(row["current_bytes"]/row["count"], 2))\nprint("Average includes the list; it is not a universal standalone instance size")',
  'Run the complete library on a small fixture and confirm its final evidence.',
  'from lab import write_report, file_digest\nr = write_report("milestone", count=101, memory_count=1000)\nprint("Parsed / selected:", r["parsed"], r["written"])\nprint("Paired digest:", r["selected_csv_sha256"] == file_digest("milestone/selected.csv"))\nprint("Streaming result and eager layout experiment have separate scopes")',
  [('Does this chart measure the streaming pipeline\'s retained memory?', 'No; it compares intentionally retained object batches', 'Always', 'Only because the count is one million', 'The two experiments have different workloads and purposes.'),
   ('May its byte difference be promised for every Python build and class?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if slots used less here', 'Layout savings depend on interpreter, inheritance and actual payload.')],
  'Publish one measured difference as universal memory saving or runtime speedup.', 'Keep the measured workload, version and allocation scope attached to the result.',
  'The bars come from actual separate-process measurements with equal counts. The library output remains independently reconstructable.',
  syntax='python lab.py --output artifacts\npython memory_lab.py --count 1000000',
  figure=('Measured live traced bytes for one million normal and slotted two-field objects', 'Equal object counts in separate Python processes; reference list and traced instance storage included, with shared zero values. This is not total RSS, a speed benchmark or a measurement of the streaming pipeline.'))
